## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, beginner-friendly plan for per-tenant log growth control without code changes; emphasizes safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Filesystem quotas
- logrotate
- tmpfiles.d cleanup
- Rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple per-container log dir monitor (mock)
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
const threshold = 500*1024*1024;
const containers = ['ct1','ct2','ct3'];
containers.forEach(ct=>{
  const dir = `/var/log/${ct}`;
  try {
    const out = execSync(`du -sb ${dir}`).toString().trim();
    const size = parseInt(out.split('\t')[0],10);
    if(size>threshold) console.log(`${ct} over limit: ${size}`);
  } catch(e){}
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the rollback plan in a staging environment?
- What metrics would you collect to verify no regression after changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of Linux memory isolation, NUMA binding, and low-latency multitenant workloads under fragmentation pressure.

## Key Concepts
- memcg v2 quotas, cpuset, NUMA bindings
- HugePages/THP strategy; allocator isolation (jemalloc arenas)
- kernel memory.compaction tuning; vmscan knobs
- per-tenant BPF observability; staged rollouts

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal eBPF: count major page allocations per cgroup id
#include <uapi/linux/ptrace.h>
BPF_HASH(page_allocs, u64, u64);
int trace_alloc(struct trace_event_raw_alloc *e) {
  u64 cid = bpf_get_current_uid_gid();
  page_allocs.increment(cid);
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify fragmentation impact and rollback criteria?
- Which tenants get priority during compaction?
